    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most conversations online never get past the first line. If you have ever wondered why your messages go unanswered, the problem is usually not you. It is the opener. These first message ideas are built around one simple truth: people reply to messages that are easy and pleasant to answer, and they ignore the ones that make them work.</p>
<p>Below you will find five openers that work, real examples to adapt, the mistakes that get messages ignored, and a few habits that keep a chat going once it has started. Everything here applies whether you are looking for a partner, a date or a new friend in your town.</p>

<h2>Why most first messages fail</h2>
<p>Think about the person on the other end. They may have received a dozen messages today, and most of them look the same: "hi", "hey beautiful", "how are you?", "can we be friends?". None of these say anything about them, and none are fun to answer. Replying to "hey" means starting a conversation from nothing, so many people simply do not.</p>
<p>A good first message does three jobs at once. It shows you actually looked at their profile, it gives them something easy to talk about, and it lets them see a little of your personality. If your message does those three things in two or three lines, you are already ahead of most people.</p>

<h2>Pick something from their profile</h2>
<p>This is the most reliable opener there is. Read their bio and look at their photos and interests, then choose one real detail and ask about it. It proves you are not sending the same line to everyone.</p>
<ul>
<li>"You mentioned hiking in Ngong. Which trail is your favourite?"</li>
<li>"Your photo at the coast looks great. Was that Diani or Watamu?"</li>
<li>"I saw you love baking. What is the one thing you make that always goes first?"</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice that each example asks about something the person enjoys talking about. People like to share what they love, so these questions are a pleasure to answer. Avoid commenting only on looks. A compliment on a smile or an outfit is fine, but pair it with a question, otherwise it is easy to thank you and stop there.</p>

<h2>Ask a light either-or question</h2>
<p>Either-or questions are quick to answer, which is why they work so well, especially for people who find small talk tiring. Instead of asking something broad, give two simple choices.</p>
<ul>
<li>"Coffee or tea in the morning? I need to know before we go any further."</li>
<li>"Beach weekend or mountain weekend?"</li>
<li>"Nyama choma on Saturday or a quiet movie night?"</li>
</ul>
<p>The answer takes five seconds, and the follow-up writes itself. If they say beach, you ask which beach. If they say tea, you ask masala or plain. A small question like this gets the ball rolling without any pressure.</p>

<h2>Share something small about yourself</h2>
<p>A conversation should feel like a two-way street. After you ask a question, it helps to offer a little of yourself too, so the other person is not being interviewed. Mention a film you watched, a place you tried, or a song you cannot stop playing, and then ask what they think.</p>
<p>For example: "I finally tried the new walking trail in Karura this weekend and I am still sore. Do you have a favourite place to get outside in the city?" That message tells them something real about you, shows you are active, and ends with a question. It is warm, specific and easy to answer.</p>

<h2>Be playful, not clever</h2>
<p>A little humour makes you memorable, but try-hard jokes or pick-up lines usually backfire. The goal is to be kind and light, not to perform. A gentle, self-aware joke works better than a line you found online.</p>
<ul>
<li>"I have been staring at this message box for ten minutes trying to sound interesting. How am I doing so far?"</li>
<li>"Quick poll: is it acceptable to put pineapple on pizza? Your answer decides everything."</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep jokes friendly and never at their expense. If you are not naturally funny, that is fine. Warmth and curiosity are just as attractive, and being yourself will serve you better over time than playing a character.</p>

<h2>Keep it short</h2>
<p>A long first message can feel heavy, like a lot of effort to match. Two or three lines is the sweet spot. You are not trying to tell your life story, only to open a door. Save the longer stories for when they have replied and the chat is flowing.</p>
<p>Also check your spelling and punctuation before you send. You do not need perfect grammar, but a message that is easy to read shows care. Avoid sending several messages in a row before they have replied, because it can feel pushy and it makes it harder for them to know where to start.</p>

<h2>First message ideas to skip</h2>
<p>Some openers almost never work, and a few can make a poor first impression or even make someone uncomfortable. Skip these:</p>
<ul>
<li>A bare "hi", "hey" or "hello" with nothing else.</li>
<li>Comments only about someone's body.</li>
<li>Asking for their phone number, WhatsApp or location in the first message.</li>
<li>Copy-and-paste lines that could be sent to anyone.</li>
<li>Anything that asks for money, favours or personal details such as where they live or work.</li>
</ul>
<p>The last point matters for safety as well as manners. Anyone who asks for money or private details early on is a warning sign. Read our guide on <a href="/blog/romance-scams-in-kenya-warning-signs">romance scams in Kenya</a> to learn what to look for.</p>

<h2>How to keep the conversation going</h2>
<p>Once they reply, your job is to keep things relaxed and curious. Answer their question properly, then ask one of your own. Follow the thread they gave you instead of jumping to a new topic. If they say they love cooking, ask what they cooked last, not what their job is.</p>
<p>Watch the balance. If you are always the one asking, add something about yourself. If you are always the one answering, ask something back. When the chat is going well after a few days, you can suggest moving to a call or meeting somewhere public. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-start-a-conversation-online">starting and keeping a conversation online</a> goes deeper, and the <a href="/blog/meet-someone-safely">checklist for meeting someone safely</a> will help when you are ready.</p>

<h2>What to do if you do not get a reply</h2>
<p>Sooner or later, a message will go unanswered. This is normal and it is almost never personal. People get busy, lose interest, or simply do not log in often. Send one friendly follow-up after a few days if you really want to, and then let it go.</p>
<p>The best use of your energy is to send a better message to the next person and to make your own profile as inviting as possible. Add clear photos, write a bio that sounds like you, and pick a few interests so others have something to ask you about. You can <a href="/browse">browse people near you</a> any time, or <a href="/register">create your free profile</a> in a couple of minutes and try one of these openers today.</p>
